# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: Zheng He's voyages 1405 to 1433, Dias 1488, Columbus 1492,
# da Gama at Calicut 1498, Magellan's fleet 20 September 1519, the Victoria home 6 September 1522.
lesson history-ships-and-maps v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=7 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Ships and the maps they drew"
  goal "Follow great voyages on the world map, put them in order, and say why sailors went so far."

  look {
    scene 36x17 {
      worldmap map names=0 blank=-1 oceans=1 places=["Nanjing", "Calicut", "Malindi"] numbered=0 route=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Between 1405 and 1433 the Chinese admiral Zheng He led seven great fleets from China across
      the Indian Ocean to India, Arabia and East Africa. Later, sailors from Europe looked for sea
      routes to Asia: Dias rounded the south of Africa in 1488, and da Gama reached India in 1498.
      In 1519 a fleet led by Magellan set out west from Spain, and in 1522 one ship, the Victoria,
      came home, the first to sail round the world. Each voyage made the maps more complete.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.voyage-route k=0
    }
    show history.voyage-route k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.voyage-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.voyage-years k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.ocean-between k=4 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show history.voyage-route k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.voyage-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.voyage-years k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.voyage-years k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show history.voyage-order k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-sail k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.voyage-months k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Zheng He crossed the Indian Ocean from 1405; the first voyage round the world came home in 1522."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that long voyages joined the world's coasts and filled in its maps, and that they
    were made from more than one side: China's fleets crossed the Indian Ocean long before Europe's
    ships reached it. The way in follows Zheng He's route; the core follows the voyage round the
    world, orders the voyages, and counts the years between them. The voyages also brought conquest
    and harm to many peoples who met the sailors; the lesson follows the ships and the maps, and you
    may want to talk about who was already living on the coasts they reached. The wrong idea to
    watch for is that the places were "discovered" as if empty. If the child is stuck, trace each
    route with a finger. Off the page, find a map of your country's coast and the ships that came to
    it. Russia's history programme for grade six covers China and India in the Middle Ages and ends
    its world strand with «Появление европейских завоевателей» in the Americas. England's key stage
    2 allows a theme beyond 1066, of which voyages are a common one.
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds the ocean Zheng He crossed, the voyages to order are far apart, and the years counted are short spans."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the route questions ask about continents rounded, the voyages are close together and muddled, the years counted run from Zheng He to Magellan, and the three-star question counts whole months between two dates in different Septembers."
  }
}
